  // Strategy (payments)
  Checkout c1(std::make_unique<CardPayment>());
  c1.confirm(101, 15.0);

  // State (booking)
  Booking b(std::make_unique<Select•ing>());
  b.print(); b.reserve(); b.print(); b.pay(); b.print();

  // Observer (notify)
  Notifier n;
  n.subscribe([](auto& m){ std::cout << "Email: " << m << "\n"; });
  n.subscribe([](auto& m){ std::cout << "SMS: " << m << "\n"; });
  n.emit("Your booking is confirmed.");

  // Facade (book flow)
  BookingFacade svc;
  svc.book(55, {10,11,12}, 30.0, 2024);

  // Flyweight (seat types)
  SeatTypeFactory f;
  auto vip = f.get("VIP", 12.0);
  Seat s{5, 8, vip, 3.0};
  std::cout << "Seat price: " << s.price() << "\n";
}
 
 

Notes for enterprise environments 

    Payments and PII: enforce PCI-DSS, tokenize card data, don’t log sensitive fields, and segregate secrets. Verify any third-party payment or messaging SDK aligns with Oracle security and compliance guidelines before use.
    Concurrency: seat locking should be atomic with short expiration; consider optimistic locking or distributed locks if horizontally scaled.
    Idempotency: make reserve/pay/cancel idempotent with request IDs to avoid double charges.
    Observability: use decorators or proxies for logging/metrics/circuit breaking around external calls.
    Configurability: use factories and strategy to switch providers and pricing rules per region or tenant without code changes.
     

